'''''Asplenium marinum''''' is a [[fern]] in the spleenwort genus ''[[Asplenium]]'', known as the '''sea spleenwort''' because of its preference for [[Sea|maritime]] [[habitat]]s. It occurs around the [[Atlantic Ocean]] coasts of western Europe and northwest Africa from southwest [[Norway]] west to Ireland and south to Morocco,<ref name="plantatlas">{{cite web |website=PlantAtlas |url=https://plantatlas2020.org/atlas/2cd4p9h.y90 |title=Sea Spleenwort ''Asplenium marinum'' L. |accessdate=2026-06-25}}</ref> and the coasts of the western [[Mediterranean Sea]] east to the [[Galite Islands]] of Tunisia, and [[Malta]].<ref>{{Cite journal|first1=Michel |last1=Muracciole |first2=Michel Jean |last2=Delaugerre |first3=Daniel |last3=Pavon|date=January 2010|title=''Asplenium marinum'' L., une fougère nouvelle pour la flore de Tunisie|url=https://www.researchgate.net/publication/272021959|journal=Poiretia, revue naturaliste du maghreb|volume=2|issn=2105-0503}}</ref> In Britain, it is most common on western coasts, and is absent in the southeast between Yorkshire and Sussex.<ref name="plantatlas"/>

It is an evergreen fern with dark green pinnate fronds 8–50&nbsp;cm long, with a thick, slightly leathery texture; the pinnae are 1.5–4&nbsp;cm long. It grown on sea cliffs, usually in crevices, often within the salt water spray zone; only very rarely inland, and inland plants rarely lasting long.<ref name="Hyde">Hyde, H. A., Wade, A. E., & Harrison, S. G. (1978). ''Welsh Ferns''. National Museum of Wales {{ISBN|0-7200-0210-9}}, pages 135–136.</ref><ref name="Streeter">{{cite book |last1=Streeter |first1=David |last2=Hart-Davies |first2=Christina |title=Collins flower guide: the most complete guide to the flowers of Britain and Europe |publisher=HarperCollins |publication-place=London |date=2009 |isbn=978-0-00-718389-0 |pages=30–31}}</ref><ref name="plantatlas"/>

It was first discovered by the early English botanist [[Thomas Johnson (botanist)|Thomas Johnson]] in 1639, who found it on his tour of Wales on [[Ynys Llanddwyn]];<ref name="Hyde"/> he named it as ''Chamae-filix marina Anglica'' on page 9 of the 1641 supplement of his ''Mercurii Botanica'', ''pars altera''.<ref>{{cite web |title=Mercurius botanicus. Sive plantarum gratiâ suscepti itineris, anno MDCXXXIV descriptio ... Ejusdem Mercurii botanici pars altera. Sive itineris in Camriam descriptio. |website=Internet Archive |date=2016-10-23 |url=https://archive.org/details/b2929020x/page/9/mode/1up |access-date=2026-06-26}}</ref> In 1753, [[Carl Linnaeus|Linnaeus]] included it in his ''[[Species Plantarum]]'', giving it a formal [[binomial nomenclature|binomial name]], ''Asplenium marinum'', which remains valid.<ref>{{cite book | title=Species Plantarum | edition=1st | volume=II | last=Linnaeus | first=C. | authorlink=Carl Linnaeus | year=1753 | publisher=Laurentii Salvii | location=Stockholm | url=https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/page/359102|page=1081}}</ref> In 1875, [[John Smith (botanist)|John Smith]] selected it as the [[type species]] of the genus ''[[Asplenium]]'' in his ''Historia filicum''.<ref>{{cite web |title=''Asplenium'' |website=International Plant Names Index |url=https://www.ipni.org/n/328226-2 |access-date=2026-06-26}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Historia filicum |website=Internet Archive |date=2016-10-23 |url=https://archive.org/details/historiafilicume00smitrich/page/316/mode/1up |access-date=2026-06-26}}</ref>

